{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787149396,"nonce":"nV3EUsQDl+CNyeORjD4B/pK4dkg=","message":"**A fifth thing to observe, and it is now load-bearing for a change that has already landed.**\n\n**Does a section block's TEXT render in the in-channel `thread_broadcast` copy?**\n\nADR 0020 Amendment 4 has three data points and none of them answers this. It establishes that the attachment is returned intact by `conversations.history` (data point 2), that the **colour bar** renders (data point 3), and that the **buttons do not** (data point 3). Amendment 4 is also explicit that data point 2 proves nothing about rendering: *\"`blocks` being present in the stored attachment (data point 2) is storage, not rendering.\"* So whether the section block a reader would actually read — the card body — appears in the channel copy has never been observed.\n\nThat gap did not matter until now. `68653ca` (landed in `43ee219`/`17f8055`) puts oto's only channel-facing affordance **in that section block**: a broadcasting reply's body now ends `— \u003curl|open in oto\u003e`, because 5b confirms a button is impossible in the in-channel copy and a mrkdwn link is the only affordance left. The inference that it reaches the reader is strong — if blocks did not render, the in-channel copy would be a sentence and a colour bar and nothing else, and the person who looked would have said so rather than carefully distinguishing buttons from colour — **but it is an inference, and this ticket exists because this ADR once built on a documented claim nobody had checked.**\n\n**What to look for, concretely:** post a broadcasting reply, then look at the message in the CHANNEL (not in the thread) and answer three things. Is the body text there at all? Is the `open in oto` link there and clickable? And does it look like a link, or like raw `\u003chttps://…|open in oto\u003e` — because the in-channel reference is a different render path and mrkdwn is not guaranteed to be re-parsed on it.\n\n**If the answer is no,** the link moves to the top-level `text`, and `TestABroadcastTopLevelTextCarriesSeverityAndDuration` — which asserts `!strings.Contains(text, \"\u003chttp\")` — is the one test that has to be amended to allow it. Named here so nobody has to go looking. ADR 0020 Amendment 5 records the same escape route.\n\n⚠️ **And note what this ticket cannot currently exercise.** `refired` has no producer (`internal/notification/domain/broadcast.go:71`, *\"⛔ RETAINED FOR HISTORY; NOTHING PRODUCES IT\"*), and `broadcast_on_resolved` is default-off. So observing any broadcast at all now requires switching `broadcast_on_resolved` on first and driving a resolve. That is one line of setup in a checklist, and without it the observation silently tests nothing.","files":null}]}